1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,591
In 1548, a young French law student named

2
00:00:03,591 --> 00:00:06,828
Étienne de La Boétie asked a question

3
00:00:06,828 --> 00:00:09,911
that still has no comfortable answer.

4
00:00:10,561 --> 00:00:13,319
How is it, he wrote, that so many people,

5
00:00:13,319 --> 00:00:16,077
so many towns, so many nations, sometimes

6
00:00:16,077 --> 00:00:17,957
put up with a single ruler,

7
00:00:18,607 --> 00:00:20,519
who has no power except the power they

8
00:00:20,519 --> 00:00:21,171
give him?

9
00:00:21,821 --> 00:00:24,537
A tyrant has only two eyes, two hands and

10
00:00:24,537 --> 00:00:27,315
one body. He can't arrest a million people

11
00:00:27,315 --> 00:00:28,179
by himself.

12
00:00:28,829 --> 00:00:31,475
He needs police, soldiers, clerks, and the

13
00:00:31,475 --> 00:00:33,592
quiet obedience of everyone else.

14
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:35,535
So why do we give it?

15
00:00:36,185 --> 00:00:38,810
This isn't a question about one country or

16
00:00:38,810 --> 00:00:41,376
one century. It's a question about human

17
00:00:41,376 --> 00:00:42,018
nature.

18
00:00:42,668 --> 00:00:45,169
And psychology, history and philosophy

19
00:00:45,169 --> 00:00:47,304
have some uncomfortable answers.

20
00:00:47,954 --> 00:00:50,270
If you like big questions about human

21
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:51,601
nature, subscribe.

22
00:00:51,601 --> 00:00:53,569
It genuinely helps the channel.

23
00:00:54,219 --> 00:00:56,253
The word dictator didn't start as an

24
00:00:56,253 --> 00:00:56,749
insult.

25
00:00:57,399 --> 00:00:59,945
In the Roman Republic, a dictator was an

26
00:00:59,945 --> 00:01:01,077
emergency office.

27
00:01:01,727 --> 00:01:04,302
In a crisis, like a war, the Senate could

28
00:01:04,302 --> 00:01:06,643
appoint one person with extraordinary

29
00:01:06,643 --> 00:01:07,228
powers,

30
00:01:07,878 --> 00:01:10,154
but only for a maximum of six months.

31
00:01:10,804 --> 00:01:13,278
The legend of Cincinnatus says he was

32
00:01:13,278 --> 00:01:15,930
called from his farm to save Rome, won the

33
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:16,342
war,

34
00:01:16,992 --> 00:01:19,463
and gave his power back within days, then

35
00:01:19,463 --> 00:01:21,260
went home to plow his fields.

36
00:01:21,910 --> 00:01:23,677
For centuries, the system worked.

37
00:01:24,327 --> 00:01:26,947
Then, in the first century BC, generals

38
00:01:26,947 --> 00:01:28,819
began to stretch the rules.

39
00:01:29,469 --> 00:01:32,328
In 44 BC, Julius Caesar was named

40
00:01:32,328 --> 00:01:34,059
dictator for life.

41
00:01:34,709 --> 00:01:37,271
A month later, he was assassinated, but

42
00:01:37,271 --> 00:01:39,590
the Republic never truly recovered.

43
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,468
The lesson Rome learned too late:

44
00:01:42,468 --> 00:01:45,068
emergency powers have a way of becoming

45
00:01:45,068 --> 00:01:45,873
permanent.

46
00:01:46,523 --> 00:01:48,350
Why would people want to give power

47
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:48,716
away?

48
00:01:49,366 --> 00:01:52,284
In 1941, in the middle of the Second World

49
00:01:52,284 --> 00:01:54,878
War, the psychologist and philosopher

50
00:01:54,878 --> 00:01:57,472
Erich Fromm published a book called

51
00:01:57,472 --> 00:01:59,093
Escape from Freedom.

52
00:01:59,743 --> 00:02:01,438
His idea was surprising:

53
00:02:01,438 --> 00:02:03,258
freedom isn't only a gift.

54
00:02:03,258 --> 00:02:04,765
It's also a burden.

55
00:02:05,415 --> 00:02:07,882
When you're free, you have to choose, take

56
00:02:07,882 --> 00:02:10,350
responsibility, and live with uncertainty.

57
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,841
In times of crisis, unemployment and fear,

58
00:02:13,841 --> 00:02:16,050
that burden can feel unbearable.

59
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:18,866
And a strong leader offers an escape:

60
00:02:18,866 --> 00:02:20,924
don't worry, I'll decide for you.

61
00:02:21,574 --> 00:02:24,030
Centuries earlier, the philosopher Thomas

62
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:26,323
Hobbes had argued something similar in

63
00:02:26,323 --> 00:02:27,141
Leviathan:

64
00:02:27,791 --> 00:02:30,093
people accept an all-powerful ruler

65
00:02:30,093 --> 00:02:32,395
because, without one, life would be

66
00:02:32,395 --> 00:02:34,273
"nasty, brutish, and short."

67
00:02:34,923 --> 00:02:37,139
Security, in exchange for freedom.

68
00:02:37,789 --> 00:02:39,958
It's a deal that looks reasonable when

69
00:02:39,958 --> 00:02:42,339
you're afraid. The problem is what happens

70
00:02:42,339 --> 00:02:43,662
after the fear passes.

71
00:02:44,312 --> 00:02:46,644
Political psychologist Karen Stenner

72
00:02:46,644 --> 00:02:49,090
found something important about how this

73
00:02:49,090 --> 00:02:49,602
works.

74
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:52,653
She argued that some people have an

75
00:02:52,653 --> 00:02:54,801
authoritarian predisposition:

76
00:02:54,801 --> 00:02:57,581
a stronger preference for sameness, order

77
00:02:57,581 --> 00:02:58,656
and obedience.

78
00:02:59,306 --> 00:03:00,513
But here's the key:

79
00:03:00,513 --> 00:03:02,598
this tendency often lies dormant.

80
00:03:03,248 --> 00:03:05,500
What activates it is a feeling of

81
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,879
threat, especially the sense that

82
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:10,507
society is falling apart, or that "our"

83
00:03:10,507 --> 00:03:12,447
way of life is under attack.

84
00:03:13,097 --> 00:03:15,534
When people feel threatened, they become

85
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:17,858
more willing to accept strong leaders,

86
00:03:17,858 --> 00:03:20,409
harsh rules and limits on others' freedom.

87
00:03:21,059 --> 00:03:23,757
That's why would-be authoritarians so

88
00:03:23,757 --> 00:03:25,443
often need an enemy:

89
00:03:25,443 --> 00:03:28,411
a crisis, a scapegoat, a danger that only

90
00:03:28,411 --> 00:03:29,558
they can stop.

91
00:03:30,208 --> 00:03:32,367
Fear isn't just a side effect of

92
00:03:32,367 --> 00:03:33,600
authoritarianism.

93
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,080
Often, it's the fuel.

94
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:37,153
Fear opens the door.

95
00:03:37,153 --> 00:03:39,009
Repetition keeps it open.

96
00:03:39,659 --> 00:03:42,429
In 1977, psychologists Lynn Hasher,

97
00:03:42,429 --> 00:03:45,267
David Goldstein and Thomas Toppino

98
00:03:45,267 --> 00:03:48,175
asked people to rate whether statements

99
00:03:48,175 --> 00:03:49,698
were true or false.

100
00:03:50,348 --> 00:03:52,436
Some statements appeared more than once

101
00:03:52,436 --> 00:03:53,530
over several weeks.

102
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,382
The result: people rated repeated

103
00:03:56,382 --> 00:03:58,700
statements as more likely to be true,

104
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:01,134
whether they were actually true or not.

105
00:04:01,784 --> 00:04:04,172
Psychologists call this the illusory

106
00:04:04,172 --> 00:04:06,560
truth effect. A familiar claim feels

107
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:09,187
easier to process, and our brains mistake

108
00:04:09,187 --> 00:04:10,440
"easy" for "true."

109
00:04:11,090 --> 00:04:13,695
Every authoritarian regime has understood

110
00:04:13,695 --> 00:04:14,998
this instinctively.

111
00:04:14,998 --> 00:04:17,425
Control the newspapers, the radio, the

112
00:04:17,425 --> 00:04:18,076
screens,

113
00:04:18,726 --> 00:04:21,396
repeat the same few messages everywhere,

114
00:04:21,396 --> 00:04:23,817
and over time they stop feeling like

115
00:04:23,817 --> 00:04:26,611
opinions. They start feeling like reality.

116
00:04:27,261 --> 00:04:29,721
But dictators don't just need followers.

117
00:04:29,721 --> 00:04:32,066
They need ordinary people willing to

118
00:04:32,066 --> 00:04:33,210
carry out orders.

119
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:37,166
In 1961, at Yale University, psychologist

120
00:04:37,166 --> 00:04:40,172
Stanley Milgram ran one of the most

121
00:04:40,172 --> 00:04:42,651
famous experiments in history.

122
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:45,698
Volunteers were told to give electric

123
00:04:45,698 --> 00:04:48,095
shocks to a "learner" in another room

124
00:04:48,095 --> 00:04:50,791
whenever he made a mistake, increasing the

125
00:04:50,791 --> 00:04:52,050
voltage each time.

126
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:54,976
The shocks were fake, and the learner was

127
00:04:54,976 --> 00:04:57,201
an actor. But the volunteers didn't know

128
00:04:57,201 --> 00:04:57,614
that.

129
00:04:58,264 --> 00:05:00,483
They heard him shout, complain about his

130
00:05:00,483 --> 00:05:01,980
heart, and then go silent.

131
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:04,989
In the best-known version, about

132
00:05:04,989 --> 00:05:07,819
two-thirds of participants kept going

133
00:05:07,819 --> 00:05:09,841
all the way to the maximum:

134
00:05:09,841 --> 00:05:10,718
450 volts.

135
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:14,066
Not because they were cruel, but because a

136
00:05:14,066 --> 00:05:16,705
calm man in a lab coat kept saying, "The

137
00:05:16,705 --> 00:05:19,284
experiment requires that you continue."

138
00:05:19,934 --> 00:05:22,467
Later researchers have questioned parts of

139
00:05:22,467 --> 00:05:23,649
Milgram's methods.

140
00:05:24,299 --> 00:05:27,016
But the core finding has been repeated in

141
00:05:27,016 --> 00:05:29,363
many forms: ordinary people will do

142
00:05:29,363 --> 00:05:32,081
surprising things when an authority takes

143
00:05:32,081 --> 00:05:33,748
responsibility for them.

144
00:05:34,398 --> 00:05:36,529
Here's what surprises most people:

145
00:05:36,529 --> 00:05:39,062
many dictatorships don't begin with tanks

146
00:05:39,062 --> 00:05:40,041
in the street.

147
00:05:40,691 --> 00:05:44,405
In January 1933, Adolf Hitler was legally

148
00:05:44,405 --> 00:05:47,359
appointed Chancellor of Germany.

149
00:05:48,009 --> 00:05:50,270
Two months later, the parliament passed

150
00:05:50,270 --> 00:05:52,638
the Enabling Act, giving his government

151
00:05:52,638 --> 00:05:55,060
the power to make laws without parliament.

152
00:05:55,710 --> 00:05:58,007
It was voted through, under intimidation,

153
00:05:58,007 --> 00:05:59,156
but through a vote.

154
00:05:59,806 --> 00:06:02,729
In their 2018 book How Democracies Die,

155
00:06:02,729 --> 00:06:05,718
political scientists Steven Levitsky and

156
00:06:05,718 --> 00:06:08,508
Daniel Ziblatt argue that this is the

157
00:06:08,508 --> 00:06:10,102
common pattern today:

158
00:06:10,752 --> 00:06:13,647
democracies are more often eroded step by

159
00:06:13,647 --> 00:06:16,543
step, by elected leaders, than overthrown

160
00:06:16,543 --> 00:06:17,859
in a single coup.

161
00:06:18,509 --> 00:06:20,840
Each step looks small and legal on its

162
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,113
own: a court packed here, a newspaper

163
00:06:23,113 --> 00:06:24,193
pressured there,

164
00:06:24,843 --> 00:06:27,474
an election rule changed, an "emergency"

165
00:06:27,474 --> 00:06:28,209
extended.

166
00:06:28,859 --> 00:06:31,218
You may have heard that a frog in slowly

167
00:06:31,218 --> 00:06:32,973
heating water won't jump out.

168
00:06:33,623 --> 00:06:35,743
In reality, frogs do jump out.

169
00:06:35,743 --> 00:06:38,346
It's people who are bad at noticing slow

170
00:06:38,346 --> 00:06:38,951
change.

171
00:06:39,601 --> 00:06:42,232
And once a dictatorship is in place, why

172
00:06:42,232 --> 00:06:44,801
do people stay quiet, even when most of

173
00:06:44,801 --> 00:06:46,024
them are unhappy?

174
00:06:46,674 --> 00:06:48,809
Economist Timur Kuran called it

175
00:06:48,809 --> 00:06:50,588
preference falsification:

176
00:06:50,588 --> 00:06:52,842
people hide what they really think,

177
00:06:52,842 --> 00:06:54,976
because speaking up is dangerous.

178
00:06:55,626 --> 00:06:57,736
And because everyone hides, everyone

179
00:06:57,736 --> 00:06:59,142
believes they're alone.

180
00:06:59,792 --> 00:07:03,370
On December 21, 1989, Romania's dictator

181
00:07:03,370 --> 00:07:06,782
Nicolae Ceaușescu gave a speech to a

182
00:07:06,782 --> 00:07:09,028
huge crowd in Bucharest.

183
00:07:09,678 --> 00:07:12,266
Partway through, some people began to boo.

184
00:07:12,266 --> 00:07:14,738
On live television, you can see his face

185
00:07:14,738 --> 00:07:15,313
freeze.

186
00:07:15,963 --> 00:07:17,855
In that moment, thousands of people

187
00:07:17,855 --> 00:07:19,747
discovered that they weren't alone.

188
00:07:20,397 --> 00:07:22,996
The very next day, he fled the capital by

189
00:07:22,996 --> 00:07:25,358
helicopter, and his regime collapsed.

190
00:07:26,008 --> 00:07:28,695
Dictatorships often look unbreakable right

191
00:07:28,695 --> 00:07:31,322
up until the moment they suddenly aren't.

192
00:07:31,972 --> 00:07:35,942
Which brings us back to La Boétie's answer

193
00:07:35,942 --> 00:07:37,089
from 1548.

194
00:07:37,739 --> 00:07:40,407
He argued that tyranny doesn't need to be

195
00:07:40,407 --> 00:07:41,681
defeated by force.

196
00:07:41,681 --> 00:07:44,288
It only needs to lose the cooperation it

197
00:07:44,288 --> 00:07:45,137
depends on.

198
00:07:45,787 --> 00:07:48,233
"Resolve to serve no more," he wrote, "and

199
00:07:48,233 --> 00:07:49,646
you are at once freed."

200
00:07:50,296 --> 00:07:52,164
Modern research points in a similar

201
00:07:52,164 --> 00:07:52,804
direction.

202
00:07:53,454 --> 00:07:55,786
Political scientist Erica Chenoweth

203
00:07:55,786 --> 00:07:58,118
studied hundreds of campaigns against

204
00:07:58,118 --> 00:08:00,392
governments in the twentieth century

205
00:08:01,042 --> 00:08:03,697
and found that nonviolent mass movements

206
00:08:03,697 --> 00:08:06,290
succeeded more often than violent ones,

207
00:08:06,290 --> 00:08:08,821
largely because they could draw in far

208
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:10,117
more participants.

209
00:08:10,767 --> 00:08:13,201
Power, in the end, is a relationship.

210
00:08:13,201 --> 00:08:15,695
Every regime, however strong it looks,

211
00:08:15,695 --> 00:08:18,128
runs on the daily choices of ordinary

212
00:08:18,128 --> 00:08:18,737
people.

213
00:08:19,387 --> 00:08:21,827
Try this. Think of a time you went along

214
00:08:21,827 --> 00:08:24,267
with something you didn't agree with, at

215
00:08:24,267 --> 00:08:26,481
school, at work, in a group, because

216
00:08:26,481 --> 00:08:28,013
everyone else seemed to.

217
00:08:28,663 --> 00:08:31,234
Ask yourself: did I actually know what the

218
00:08:31,234 --> 00:08:33,520
others thought? Or did I just assume?

219
00:08:34,170 --> 00:08:36,844
Very often, the silence around you is made

220
00:08:36,844 --> 00:08:39,161
of people who feel exactly like you.

221
00:08:39,811 --> 00:08:42,271
We don't let dictators rise because we're

222
00:08:42,271 --> 00:08:43,333
evil, or stupid.

223
00:08:43,983 --> 00:08:46,242
We let them rise because we're afraid,

224
00:08:46,242 --> 00:08:48,446
because we're tired, because we trust

225
00:08:48,446 --> 00:08:50,650
authority, and because we think we're

226
00:08:50,650 --> 00:08:51,146
alone.

227
00:08:51,796 --> 00:08:53,369
Those are human weaknesses.

228
00:08:53,369 --> 00:08:55,415
But they come with a human strength:

229
00:08:56,065 --> 00:08:58,535
the power was always ours to give, and

230
00:08:58,535 --> 00:08:59,801
ours to take back.

